# ~/.gdbinit — load KernLens in every GDB session.
#
# Typical checkout:
#   source /home/bogon/workSpaces/kernlens/gdbinit.py
#
# Replace the path below with the actual checkout (or an installed
# gdbinit.py).  Load other GDB extensions first if desired;
# KernLens uses the `kernlens-` command prefix and does not install
# breakpoints at load time.
#
# Commands are available as both `kernlens-*` and `kl-*`, for example:
#   kl-help                 # list every command, grouped by category
#   kl-help kl-<command>    # usage, options, and examples for one
#   kl-status
#   kl-slab <list|info|contains|details> [arg]
#   kl-page <address>
#   kl-pte <page-table-entry> [virtual-address]
#   kl-sym <address>
#   kl-bt [depth]
#   kl-chain [depth]
#   kl-read <address> [count]
#   kl-kvar <symbol>
#   kl-addr <address> [tag-mask]
#   kl-pt <virtual-address>
#   kl-walk <virtual-address> [4|5]
#   kl-bits <value> [bit-index]
#   kl-int <value> [width]
#   kl-cpu [cpu]
#   kl-percpu <symbol> [cpu]
#   kl-base
#   kl-kbase [--rebase] [--verbose]
# The startup file also installs the public Python API into GDB's
# __main__ namespace.  A script sourced later can use KAddr,
# KPageTable, IntFmt, kdbg, read_kvar, walk, byte_swap64, ... without
# imports.  Class-qualified forms such as BitOps.byte_swap64 and
# BitOps_byte_swap64 avoid collisions; logger, styler, reader, and
# other stateful components are default kdbg instances:
#   (gdb) source /path/to/KernLens/examples/trace.py
# `trace.py` is an example; replace it with your own tracer.
source /path/to/KernLens/gdbinit.py